343: A rational function on \(\mathbb P^1\) whose divisor is
344: divisible by \(b\) has a rational b-th root: factor its zeros
345: and poles, divide their integer orders by \(b\), and choose a
346: b-th root of its nonzero constant. Choose this root \(g_0\)
347: with the specified cusp value. Then the actual ordinary endpoint
348: map is
349: \[
350:                     D_r=r^{-\varepsilon M/b}g_0(t)U^\varepsilon.
351:                                                                \tag{WS.14}
352: \]
353: The exponent is an integer. On the cap root, its apparent power
354: of \(z\) is \(-Mn_i/b\). The actual ordinary-to-cap parameter
355: gluing changes by \(+Mn_i\log z\,\delta/(2\pi ib)\)
356: under \(r\mapsto\zeta_b r\). This cancels that power exactly.
357: The remaining map is an invariant unit times \(L_i^\varepsilon\),
358: and is therefore invertible through every root component. Its
359: conjugation is exactly
360: \[
361:  L_i^\varepsilon A_{i,r}L_i^{-\varepsilon}
362:       =\chi_i^{-\varepsilon}A_{i,r}
363:       =\zeta_b^{P_i}A_{i,r}=A_{i,\zeta_b r}.            \tag{WS.15}
364: \]
365: The same map commutes with \(B_\lambda\), since
366: \(\lambda=(\zeta_b r)^M=r^M\). This is the exact quotient
367: map on the actual cap, with no lost finite character.
368: 
369: The weighted Jacobi marking checks the same map integrally.
370: For a source monodromy \(E\) and its marked normal-function
371: column \(k\), the actual cohomology lift is
372: \[
373:  T(E,k,z)=\begin{pmatrix}
374:  1&b(E_{21}k_1-E_{11}k_2)&b(E_{22}k_1-E_{12}k_2)&z\\
375:  0&E_{11}&E_{12}&ak_1\\
376:  0&E_{21}&E_{22}&ak_2\\
377:  0&0&0&1
378:  \end{pmatrix}.
379: \]
380: For \(A=T^{-t}\), literal multiplication gives
381: \[
382:  (A-I)e_\gamma=(0,bk_2,-bk_1,-z)^t.
383: \]
384: Thus the one-step lattice cochain is
385: \[
386:  \frac{P_i\delta-\varepsilon(A_i-I)e_\gamma}{b}
387:     =(0,-\varepsilon k_2,\varepsilon k_1,
388:                      (P_i+\varepsilon z_i)/b)^t.
389:                                                                \tag{WS.16}
390: \]
391: Its integrality is precisely (WS.15). The source columns in
392: this formula are retained integral source periods; the last
393: entry is supplied by the actual line commutator above.
394: 
395: ## 5. Exact cyclic norm and simultaneous closed Cox origin
396: 
397: At the selected I1 choose the actual bounded Tate lift of \(G\).
398: The outward \(bG\) period then has source unit its b-th power.
399: This fixes its logarithm \(\Gamma=b\mu_G\) in the marked source
400: cover. In the actual cusp notation
401: \[
402:  H=\varepsilon s+U(q),\qquad
403:  \gamma=(\Gamma,C-s-Mv),\qquad v=\frac{\log r}{2\pi i}.
404: \]
405: The endpoint (WS.14) has the bounded lift
406: \[
407:        \tau=\frac{H\delta+\varepsilon\gamma}{b}
408:           =\binom{\varepsilon\Gamma/b}
409:                   {(U+\varepsilon C-\varepsilon Mv)/b}.
410:                                                                \tag{WS.17}
411: \]
412: It multiplies every cusp Cox character by its literal bounded
413: unit, so it extends simultaneously through all three curves,
414: both triple points, and every allowed periodic fan.
415: 
416: There is a harmless but necessary scalar norm correction. Set
417: \[
418:  \alpha=\frac{\varepsilon M(b-1)}{2b^2},\qquad
419:  \widetilde D_r=e^{2\pi i\alpha}D_r.
420:                                                                \tag{WS.18}
421: \]
422: This is an actual constant physical-delta translation and changes
423: none of the cap conjugations. It must be retained even when
424: \(b=2\) and \(M/b\) is odd. Since \(v\) advances by \(1/b\),
425: direct summation gives
426: \[
427:  \sum_{j=0}^{b-1}(\tau(v+j/b)+\alpha\delta)
428:                          =b\tau(v)=H\delta+\varepsilon\gamma.
429:                                                                \tag{WS.19}
430: \]
431: On the ordinary part the same equality follows directly from
432: \(U^b\rho=B_0^{-1}\) in (WS.7) and the parameter factor
433: \(r^{-\varepsilon M/b}\). Hence the b-th iterate of the
434: endpoint is exactly the already specified one-gamma cover
435: endpoint, including its line scalar. The cyclic quotient of the
436: parameter family is taken in that order: first work over the
437: universal \(v\)-plane, where the proposed map covers
438: \(v\mapsto v+1/b\). Its b-th iterate equals the prescribed
439: \(v\mapsto v+1\) gluing map just calculated. Quotient by this
440: latter map to obtain the already constructed r-family. The first
441: map then induces an order-b action over \(r\mapsto\zeta_b r\).
442: In particular the nonconstant multiplier \(h_0\) is retained
443: as part of the r-family gluing; it is not declared the identity
444: in an unglued ordinary chart. The quotient of the
445: proper r-family by this induced deck action is therefore an actual
446: proper holomorphic family over the punctured w-disc. The action
447: is free because it rotates the nonzero parameter.
448: 
449: Use the already specified cover cusp origin
450: \[
451:  \sigma(v)=\binom{\varepsilon\Gamma v}
452:                 {v(U+\varepsilon C)-\varepsilon M(v^2-v)/2}.
453: \]
454: Its exact difference is
455: \[
456:                \sigma(v+1/b)-\sigma(v)=\tau+\alpha\delta.
457:                                                                \tag{WS.20}
458: \]
459: Thus the same origin, without changing the fan or any ray label,
460: is a closed simultaneous Cox product for the w-family. Its
461: pullback is exactly the closed r-product of K30/K32. The scalar
462: correction is physical delta; it acts on the actual quaternion
463: coordinate by right scalar multiplication and fixes the Hopf
464: normal vector. The bounded torus-unit comparison is K30's
465: literal \(q_{\rm H}\mapsto A q_{\rm H}B\); it has no free
466: normal-frame component. All initial normal and exterior markings
467: are therefore the ones used by K31, not just abstractly
468: isomorphic bundles.
469: 
470: ## 6. Integer characteristic naturality and the fan class
471: 
472: The single w-loop has the actual vertical-edge semifree circle,
473: its standard fixed sphere, and the closed normal framing just
474: constructed. Its b-fold parameter pullback is the prescribed
475: r-loop with exactly the K30/K32 framing. Consequently its
476: ordinary characteristic numbers satisfy
477: \[
478:  b k_w=-3H,\qquad b\ell_w=-24\chi.
479: \]
480: The left sides are ordinary integers of this actual mapping
481: torus. Its index is \(A_w=(4k_w-\ell_w)/24\), and the proved
482: Hopf transfer assigns the initial central class
483: \(A_w\pmod{28}\). This establishes (WS.4) by naturality on
484: the constructed covering, not by choosing a residue-class
485: quotient. Applying K31 to the simultaneous product in (WS.20)
486: then proves (WS.5).
487: 
488: The exact certificate `code/part3_fourfold/weighted_shorter_loop.py`
489: checks the full Jacobi gamma-column identity, the two I2 frame
490: compatibility equations, all twelve finite character rows over
491: their complete residue domains, and the exact origin/norm
492: identities (WS.19)--(WS.20). Its JSON and captured output are
493: retained alongside it. The explicit root-line and global
494: meromorphic maps above are the geometric proof; the modular
495: computation does not replace them.
496: 
497: The same certificate reads F53's full thirty nonempty branch
498: polynomials and proves, over \(\mathbb Z[l,k]\), that
499: \(M/b\), \(-3H/b\), \(-24\chi/b\), and
500: \((\chi-H/2)/b\) all have integer coefficients. It also checks
501: \(4k_w-\ell_w=24A_w\) identically on every branch. The
502: determinant-excluded good/good branch remains excluded. Thus
503: integrality is checked on the complete symbolic catalogue,
504: rather than on a bounded selection of root orders.
